course: biology
lesson: 184
title: Great rivers and wetlands: Nile crocodiles, hippos, giant catfish and the Pantanal
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A great river is a long road of water through the land, and wherever it spreads into swamps, lakes and floods, it becomes a wetland packed with life, from giant fish to pink flamingos.
explain: Animals plan their lives around the water: wildebeest must cross rivers to reach new grass, and Nile crocodiles wait at exactly those crossings, like someone waiting at the school gate who knows when the bell rings.
explain: Rivers hold some of the biggest freshwater fish on Earth, dolphins that find food in muddy water by echoes, and in Brazil's Pantanal, jaguars that hunt caimans along the banks.
explain: Water even moves across a desert: in Botswana the Okavango river floods in the middle of the dry season, because rain that fell far away takes months to arrive.
fact: Nile crocodiles wait at places where wildebeest and zebras cross rivers to reach new grass, and after a big meal a crocodile can go many months without eating.
fact: Hippos yawn widely to show their huge teeth as a warning; they are very territorial and are often said to kill more people in Africa than lions do.
fact: The Mekong giant catfish is one of the biggest freshwater fish, as heavy as a bear, has no teeth as an adult, eats algae and plants, and is now very rare because of dams and fishing.
fact: The Ganges river dolphin is almost blind and finds food by echolocation (making clicks and listening for their echoes), because the muddy river water makes eyes nearly useless, and it swims on its side to search the bottom.
fact: In 2022 a giant freshwater stingray of about three hundred kilograms was caught in the Mekong River in Cambodia; it was measured, tagged and released, and was reported as the biggest freshwater fish ever recorded.
fact: The Pantanal in Brazil is the world's biggest tropical wetland, where jaguars hunt caimans (cousins of crocodiles) and capybaras (giant rodents) and big animals are easier to see than in the rainforest.
fact: The shoebill is a huge bird with a beak like a shoe that stands still for hours waiting for lungfish to come up to breathe, and it clatters its beak loudly.
fact: Beavers build dams that make ponds where many other animals live, and one beaver dam in Canada is so big it can be seen in pictures taken from space.
fact: Salmon swim upstream from the sea to the river where they hatched, finding it by smell, and leftovers from bears catching salmon help feed forest trees.
fact: The Okavango river floods a desert in the middle of the dry season, because rain from far away takes months to flow down to the delta, and the flood feeds elephants, lions and birds.
fact: Flamingos get their pink colour from pink chemicals in the tiny algae and shrimps they eat, so zoo flamingos are given special food to stay pink.
exercise: Why do Nile crocodiles wait at river crossings? | Wildebeest and zebras must cross there to reach new grass | The water is warmest there, so the crocodiles can sleep in it all day | They are waiting for boats | It is where they lay eggs
exercise: How long can a Nile crocodile go without eating after a big meal? | Many months | One hour | One day exactly | It must eat every hour
exercise: What does a hippo's wide yawn usually mean? | A warning showing its huge teeth | That it is sleepy and friendly and wants a pat | That it is hungry for grass | That it wants to play
exercise: Why can hippos be dangerous to people in boats? | They are very territorial | They eat people as their main food | They are blind | They cannot swim
exercise: What does the Mekong giant catfish eat? | Algae and plants | Small crocodiles | Birds | Boats' ropes
exercise: Why is the Mekong giant catfish so rare now? | Dams and fishing | Too much sunshine | Hippos eat them all | Children catch them for pets
exercise: How does the Ganges river dolphin find food in muddy water? | By echolocation | By its sharp eyesight | By following boats | By smelling the air
exercise: How does the Ganges river dolphin search the river bottom? | It swims on its side | It walks on its fins | It digs with its tail | It sends a crab
exercise: What happened to the giant stingray caught in Cambodia in 2022? | It was measured, tagged and released | It was put in a zoo in the capital city | It was eaten at a big village feast | It was stuffed for a museum
exercise: What is the Pantanal? | The world's biggest tropical wetland, in Brazil | A huge, dry sandy desert in the far north of Africa | A mountain in Asia | A city in Spain
exercise: What do jaguars hunt in the Pantanal? | Caimans and capybaras | Penguins and seals | Polar bears and walruses on the ice | Only fruit
exercise: How does a shoebill catch lungfish? | It stands still until they come up to breathe, then strikes | It dives deep and chases them along the bottom | It sings to them | It waits for them to jump into its nest, then sits on them till they are tired
exercise: How do beaver dams help other animals? | They make ponds where many animals live | They block all the animals from getting to the water | They keep the river dry | They scare away fish
exercise: How do salmon find the river where they hatched? | By smell | By reading signs | By following the Moon | By asking bears
exercise: Why does the Okavango flood in the dry season? | Rain from far away takes months to flow down | It rains only in the dry season, every afternoon | The sea pushes water in | Elephants pour it in
exercise: Why are flamingos pink? | Pink chemicals in the algae and shrimps they eat | They are born bright pink and stay that colour all their lives | They blush when shy | The sunset paints them pink every evening
test: «hippos look so chill, can we row up close» What should you do? | No, keep far away, hippos are territorial and dangerous | Yes, they are gentle and love being stroked on the nose by people | Yes, feed them bread | Only if they yawn
test: In Fantasia, hippos dance ballet in tutus. Which is true of real hippos? | They are territorial and often said to kill more people in Africa than lions | They are shy and harmless | They live in snowy mountains | They can fly a little
test: «5 hippos in the river, how many legs» | 20 | 10 | 25 | 15
test: «12 flamingos standing on 1 leg each, how many legs in the water» | 12 | 24 | 6 | 36
test: «Okavango flood: 3 elephants came, how many trunks and tails altogether» | 6 | 3 | 12 | 9
test: What would happen to a zoo flamingo fed only on plain bread? | It would slowly lose its pink colour | It would turn bright blue | It would get pinker and pinker each year | Nothing at all
test: «crocs gotta eat every day like us» What is right? | After a big meal a Nile crocodile can go many months without eating | Right, every day | They eat every hour | Crocodiles never eat anything at all, they live on the sunshine
test: «flood in a desert in the DRY season? ur making it up» What is right? | In the Okavango, far-away rain arrives months later, in the dry season | Right, impossible | It is only a rumour | It floods with sand, not water
test: What would happen to a salmon put in the wrong river? | It might be lost, because salmon find their home river by smell | It would read a map | It would just stay out at sea and never lay any eggs anywhere at all | It would turn into a trout
test: «giant fish as big as a car? must be a whale» What is right? | It was a giant freshwater stingray caught in the Mekong in 2022 | It was a whale in a river | It was a shark from the sea | No fish that big exists
test: «bird with a shoe for a beak just standing there for hours, is it broken» | It is waiting for lungfish to come up to breathe | It is asleep for the whole winter | It is a statue | It is stuck in the mud and cannot get out until the next rain
exam: Identify the means by which the Ganges river dolphin locates its food. | Echolocation, its vision being of little use in turbid water | Acute vision in clear water | The detection of heat by its fins | The following of fishing vessels
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Pantanal? | It is the largest tropical wetland in the world, situated in Brazil | It is the largest hot desert in the world | It is a mountain range in Asia | It is a coral reef in the Indian Ocean
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the Okavango Delta is inundated during the dry season. | Precipitation falling at a great distance requires months to flow down to the delta | Rainfall occurs there exclusively in the dry season | Sea water enters the delta from the coast | The flood is caused by melting snow upon the delta
exam: State the origin of the pink colouration of flamingos. | Pigments contained in the algae and small crustaceans that they consume | A colour present invariably from hatching | Exposure to the setting sun | The blood vessels of their feathers
